{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_22","type":"agent_message","text":"One useful result is that fine-tuning can achieve strong edit scores while substantially degrading generated text. The handoff will therefore include answer changes, probability shifts, and unrelated behavior—not just whether the model prefers “5” over “4.” The specified CounterFact mirror is small enough to download in full."}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_23","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"pwd; cat >> literature_review.md <<'EOF'\n- **2402.11078, chunk 2 (pp. 4–6):** GPT-J mass CounterFact edit score 86.5 versus MEMIT 85.8; locality 72.0. Single-edit GPT-2 XL score 83.1 versus ROME 89.2. Fluency/consistency suffer; Wikipedia LM loss recovers much of generation quality. Layer-restricted FT improves locality. These scores are not evidence of universal isolation.\n- **2402.11078, chunk 3 (p. 7):** WikiRecent extension uses 1,266 edits, 18,990 paraphrases, 12,660 replay prompts. Single-edit consistency also falls (15.2 versus original 31.9). Subject-aware SWEAOS augmentation provides no improvement; routing assumptions matter.\n- **2407.12828, chunk 1 (pp. 1–3):** GradSim is cosine similarity of answer-probability gradients. RippleEdits popular/recent; ROME/MEMIT on GPT-2 XL/Llama-7B; 50 sampled generations at temperature .7, length 15. Metrics include absolute and normalized log-likelihood gain. Official code JiaxinQin0814/Ripple_Effect_Analysis.\nEOF\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
