{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_28","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"pwd; cat >> literature_review.md <<'EOF'\n- **2401.07453, chunk 8 (pp. 22–24):** Downstream F1 plots show degradation can precede catastrophic fact forgetting. Four repeated subsets strengthen the qualitative pattern but do not establish behavior for modern architectures.\n- **2401.07453, chunk 9 (pp. 25–27):** Layer-normalized distance curves: ROME step changes, MEMIT growth across edited layers, FT-C gradual growth. Parameter norms are a diagnostic, not a substitute for behavioral preservation.\n- **2401.07453, chunk 10 (pp. 28–30):** Post-collapse repeated-token generation examples; appendix gives ROME and MEMIT objectives, KL term and subject-key averaging. Arithmetic has no natural named-entity subject, so any adaptation needs explicit token-position controls.\n- **2401.07453, chunk 11 (p. 31):** MEND edit/locality losses and FT-C infinity-norm constraint; GPT-2 XL layer 18, GPT-J layer 6. These are source-model settings, not universal layer choices.\nEOF\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
