{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_30","type":"agent_message","text":"The literature supports testing three questions: how preservation losses affect leakage, how training breadth affects generalization, and how weight edits compare with explicit exceptions. It does not establish that near-perfect isolation is possible only through string matching. I’ll treat that as a hypothesis and define “belief-like” change through downstream use and robustness."}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_31","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"pwd; cat >> literature_review.md <<'EOF'\n- **2401.01286, chunk 6 (pp. 16–18):** Updated FT-M results are strong; portability remains weak. General-task evaluation after only five edits finds modest average damage, contrasting with long sequential-edit collapse; protocol differences reconcile the findings.\n- **2401.01286, chunk 7 (pp. 19–21):** Cross-domain sequential edits degrade reliability, portability, locality and fluency; error taxonomy includes repetition, missing tokens, irrelevant answers and partial replacement. Sparse parameter change need not mean sparse behavioral change.\n- **2401.01286, chunk 8 (pp. 22–24):** Localization of an entity can resemble localization of unrelated knowledge; causal tracing and optimal edit locations need not coincide. Weight sparsity and embedding-space hits are descriptive diagnostics.\n- **2401.01286, chunk 9 (pp. 25–27):** Applications in reasoning, model manipulation, multimodal editing, toxicity and bias; editing back is not necessarily inverse restoration. These applications are outside the three-direction budget.\n- **2401.01286, chunk 10 (pp. 28–30):** Privacy/personality applications and limitations; explicit uncertainty about factual knowledge versus response manipulation. Official KnowEdit and EasyEdit resource links confirmed.\nEOF\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
