{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_49","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"pwd; .venv/bin/python - <<'PY'\nimport json,collections\nx=[json.loads(l) for l in open('notes/search_downloads_retry.jsonl')];print(collections.Counter((i['status'],i.get('fallback_status')) for i in x));print(x[0]);print('\\\\n'.join(f\\\"{i['index']} {i['file']} {i['title']}\\\" for i in x if i['status']=='downloaded'))\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"/workspaces/isolating-knowledge-updates-2d1a-codex-gpt-6-astra\nCounter({('failed', 'failed'): 199, ('downloaded', 'arxiv_title_match'): 20, ('downloaded', None): 11, ('failed', 'no_matching_arxiv_title'): 3})\n{'title': 'Representation Shattering in Transformers: A Synthetic Study with Knowledge Editing', 'year': 2024, 'authors': 'Kento Nishi, Maya Okawa, Rahul Ramesh, Mikail Khona, E. Lubana, Hidenori Tanaka', 'url': 'https://api.semanticscholar.org/CorpusId:273507931', 'relevance': 3, 'abstract': 'Knowledge Editing (KE) algorithms alter models\\' weights to perform targeted updates to incorrect, outdated, or otherwise unwanted factual associations. However, recent work has shown that applying KE can adversely affect models\\' broader factual recall accuracy and diminish their reasoning abilities. Although these studies give insights into the potential harms of KE algorithms, e.g., performance evaluations on benchmarks, little is understood about why such destructive failures occur. Motivated by this, we define a novel synthetic task in which a Transformer is trained from scratch to internalize a\"structured\"knowledge graph. The structure enforces relationships between entities of the graph, such that editing a factual association has\"trickling effects\"on other entities (e.g., altering X\\'s parent is Y to Z affects who X\\'s siblings\\' parent is). Through evaluations of edited models on this task, we show that KE inadvertently affects representations of entities beyond the targeted one, distorting relevant structures that allow a model to infer unseen knowledge about an entity. We call this phenomenon representation shattering and demonstrate that it degrades models\\' factual recall and reasoning performance. We further corroborate our findings in naturalistic settings with pre-trained Llama and Mamba models as well. Overall, our work yields a precise mechanistic hypothesis to explain why KE has adverse effects on model abilities.', 'citations': 23, 'index': 0, 'status': 'downloaded', 'error': '429 Client Error:  for url: https://api.semanticscholar.org/graph/v1/paper/CorpusId:273507931?fields=title%2CexternalIds%2CopenAccessPdf', 'file': 'papers/2410.17194.pdf', 'pdf_url': 'https://arxiv.org/pdf/2410.17194v5', 'pages': 29, 'sha256': 'f29b7511307c59e1bf689e6a0a3901b6976e639a719e01df65387555cd224a6b', 'fallback_status': 'arxiv_title_match', 'arxiv_id': '2410.17194v5'}\n0 papers/2410.17194.pdf Representation Shattering in Transformers: A Synthetic Study with Knowledge Editing\n1 papers/2202.05262.pdf Locating and Editing Factual Associations in GPT\n2 papers/2206.06520.pdf Memory-Based Model Editing at Scale\n3 papers/2310.16218.pdf Knowledge Editing for Large Language Models: A Survey\n4 papers/2308.08742.pdf PMET: Precise Model Editing in a Transformer\n5 papers/2503.05212.pdf Knowledge Updating? No More Model Editing! Just Selective Contextual Reasoning\n6 papers/2412.17872.pdf Joint Knowledge Editing for Information Enrichment and Probability Promotion\n7 papers/2604.05995.pdf The Model Agreed, But Didn't Learn: Diagnosing Surface Compliance in Large Language Models\n9 papers/2508.05083.pdf MedMKEB: A Comprehensive Knowledge Editing Benchmark for Medical Multimodal Large Language Models\n10 papers/2505.18933.pdf REACT: Representation Extraction And Controllable Tuning to Overcome Overfitting in LLM Knowledge Editing\n11 papers/2306.09306.pdf Propagating Knowledge Updates to LMs Through Distillation\n12 papers/2512.16227.pdf An information-theoretic framework for robust l