{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_61","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"pwd; cat >> literature_review.md <<'EOF'\n\n### 2410.17194, chunks 9–10 (pp. 25–29; complete)\nAdditional edit visualizations; Mamba calendar distortion replicates but MMLU-Redux was not evaluated for that non-instruction model. GPT2-XL city-country tree experiment on25cities compares closest/farthest edits, average distortion ratio1.1483. Tree structure not cleanly visible, authors call evidence preliminary. All10chunks reviewed; this is strong motivation for stratified tests, not evidence specific to 2+2 or universal impossibility.\nEOF\nhead -40 notes/paper_metadata.json; head -c 1600 notes/search_downloads_retry.jsonl; cat scripts/download_datasets.py\"","aggregated_output":"/workspaces/isolating-knowledge-updates-2d1a-codex-gpt-6-astra\n[\n  {\n    \"id\": \"2307.12976\",\n    \"title\": \"Evaluating the Ripple Effects of Knowledge Editing in Language Models\",\n    \"authors\": [\n      \"Cohen, Roi\",\n      \"Biran, Eden\",\n      \"Yoran, Ori\",\n      \"Globerson, Amir\",\n      \"Geva, Mor\"\n    ],\n    \"url\": \"https://arxiv.org/abs/2307.12976\",\n    \"abstract\": \"Abstract: Modern language models capture a large body of factual knowledge. However, some facts can be incorrectly induced or become obsolete over time, resulting in factually incorrect generations. This has led to the development of various editing methods that allow updating facts encoded by the model. Evaluation of these methods has primarily focused on testing whether an individual fact has been successfully injected, and if similar predictions for other subjects have not changed. Here we argue that such evaluation is limited, since injecting one fact (e.g. ``Jack Depp is the son of Johnny Depp'') introduces a ``ripple effect'' in the form of additional facts that the model needs to update (e.g.``Jack Depp is the sibling of Lily-Rose Depp''). To address this issue, we propose a novel set of evaluation criteria that consider the implications of an edit on related facts. Using these criteria, we then construct RippleEdits, a diagnostic benchmark of 5K factual edits, capturing a variety of types of ripple effects. We evaluate prominent editing methods on RippleEdits, showing that current methods fail to introduce consistent changes in the model's knowledge. In addition, we find that a simple in-context editing baseline obtains the best scores on our benchmark, suggesting a promising research direction for model editing.\",\n    \"pages\": 15\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"2407.12828\",\n    \"title\": \"Why Does New Knowledge Create Messy Ripple Effects in LLMs?\",\n    \"authors\": [\n      \"Qin, Jiaxin\",\n      \"Zhang, Zixuan\",\n      \"Li, Manling\",\n      \"Yu, Pengfei\",\n      \"Ji, Heng\"\n    ],\n    \"url\": \"https://arxiv.org/abs/2407.12828\",\n    \"abstract\": \"Abstract: Extensive previous research has focused on post-training knowledge editing (KE) for language models (LMs) to ensure that knowledge remains accurate and up-to-date. One desired property and open question in KE is to let edited LMs correctly handle ripple effects, where LM is expected to answer its logically related knowledge accurately. In this paper, we answer the question of why most KE methods still create messy ripple effects. We conduct extensive analysis and identify a salient indicator, GradSim, that effectively reveals when and why updated knowledge ripples in LMs. GradSim is computed by the cosine similarity between gradients of the original fact and its related knowledge. We observe a strong positive correlation between ripple effect performance and GradSim across different LMs, KE methods, and evaluation metrics. Further investigations into three counter-intuitive failure cases (Negation, Over-Ripple, Multi-Lingual) of ripple effects demonstrate that these failures are often associated with very low GradSim. This finding validates that GradSim is an effective indicator of when knowledge ripples in LMs.\",\n    \"pages\": 8\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": \"2401.07453\",\n    \"title\": \"Model Editing at Scale leads to Gradual and Catastrophic Forgetting\",\n    \"authors\": [\n      \"Gupta, Akshat\",\n      \"Rao, Anurag\",\n      \"Anumanchipalli, Gopala\"\n    ],\n    \"url\": \"https://arxiv.org/abs/2401.07453\",\n    \"abstract\": \"Abstract: Editing knowledge in large language models is an attractive capability to have which allows us to correct incorrectly learnt facts during pre-training, as well as update the model with an ever-growing list of new facts. While existing model editing techniques have shown promise, they are usually evaluated using metrics for reliability, specificity and generalization over one or few edits. We argue that for model editing to have practical utility, we must be able to make multiple edits to the same model. With this in mind, we evaluate the current model editing methods at scale, focusing on two state of the art methods: ROME and MEMIT. We find that as the model is edited sequentially with multiple facts, it continually forgets previously edited facts and the ability to perform downstream tasks. This forgetting happens in two phases -- an initial gradual but progressive forgetting phase followed by abrupt or catastrophic forgetting phase. Both gradual and catastrophic forgetting limit the usefulness of model editing methods at scale -- the former making model editing less effective as multiple edits are made to the model while the latter caps the scalability of such model editing methods. Our analysis also highlights other key limitations of ROME and MEMIT at scale. With our work, we push for the development and evaluation of model editing methods keeping scalability in mind.\",\n    \"pages\": 31\n{\"title\": \"Representation Shattering in Transformers: A Synthetic Study with Knowledge Editing\", \"year\": 2024, \"authors\": \"Kento Nishi, Maya Okawa, Rahul Ramesh, Mikail Khona, E. Lubana, Hidenori Tanaka\", \"url\": \"https://api.semanticscholar.org/CorpusId:273507931\", \"relevance\": 3, \"abstract\": \"Knowledge Editing (KE) algorithms alter models' weights to perform targeted updates to incorrect, outdated, or otherwise unwanted factual associations. However, recent work has shown that applying KE can adversely affect models' broader factual recall accuracy and diminish their reasoning abilities. Although these studies give insights into the potential harms of KE algorithms, e.g., performance evaluations on benchmarks, little is understood about why such destructive failures occur. Motivated by this, we define a novel synthetic task in which a Transformer is trained from scratch to internalize a\\\"structured\\\"knowledge graph. The structure enforces relationships between entities of the graph, such that editing a factual association has\\\"trickling effects\\\"on other entities (e.g., altering X's parent is Y to Z affects who X's siblings' parent is). Through evaluations of edited models on this task, we show that KE inadvertently affects representations of entities beyond the targeted one, distorting relevant structures that allow a model to infer unseen knowledge about an entity. We call this phenomenon representation shattering and demonstrate that it degrades models' factual recall and reasoning performance. We further corroborate our findings in naturalistic settings with pre-trainefrom pathlib import Path\nimport requests,json,hashlib\nimport pyarrow.parquet as pq\nR=Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1];assert Path.cwd()==R and (R/'.git').exists()\nout=R/'datasets/counterfact';out.mkdir(exist_ok=True)\nm=json.loads((R/'notes/counterfact_hf_metadata.json').read_text());manifest=[]\nfor entry in m['siblings']:\n f=entry['rfilename']\n if not f.endswith('.parquet'):continue\n url=f\"https://huggingface.co/datasets/azhx/counterfact/resolve/{m['sha']}/{f}\"\n r=requests.get(url,timeout=120);r.raise_for_status();p=out/Path(f).name;p.write_bytes(r.content);t=pq.read_table(p);rows=t.to_pylist()\n assert all(x['requested_rewrite'] and x['paraphrase_prompts'] is not None for x in rows)\n manifest.append({'file':str(p.relative_to(R)),'source':url,'rows':t.num_rows,'bytes':p.stat().st_size,'sha256':hashlib.sha256(r.content).hexdigest(),'null_counts':{c:t[c].null_count for c in t.column_names}})\n if f.startswith('data/train'):(R/'datasets/counterfact_sample.json').write_text(json.dumps(rows[:3],indent=2))\n(R/'datasets/validation.json').write_text(json.dumps(manifest,indent=2));print(json.dumps(manifest,indent=2))\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_62","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"pwd; cat > planning.md <<'EOF'\n# Research plan: isolating a single arithmetic update\n\n## Scope and evidence\nThe question is empirical: how small can measured behavioral change be at matched edit success? No finite benchmark establishes \\\"nothing else changed\\\" for every possible input. A changed belief is operationalized by held-out semantic generalization, downstream use, and resistance to correction; these are separate measures, not a metaphysical verdict. \"'`2+2=5` is inconsistent with ordinary arithmetic axioms. Downstream tests therefore use an explicit local evaluation rule, not a claim that all familiar algebra remains coherent.\n\nSearch phrases: knowledge editing locality; counterfactual arithmetic; ripple effects; model editing fine-tuning preservation; representation shattering; synthetic document fine-tuning beliefs; arithmetic circuits; semantic generalization versus lexical memorization. Paper-finder diligent search returned 233 candidates. All were title/abstract screened where available; downloads were attempted for every entry, with arXiv fallback. Many broad results are archival, not implementation recommendations. See paper_search.json and notes/search_downloads_retry.jsonl.\n\n## Direction ranking (fixed before experiments)\nScores are 1–5: literature support E, hypothesis relevance R, expected information gain I, feasibility F. Total = E+R+I+F; ties prefer feasibility. Scores are prospective judgments, not experimental measurements.\n\n| Rank | Direction | E | R | I | F | Total | Decision and evidence |\n|---|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---|\n| 1 | Parametric isolation frontier: masked fine-tuning with preservation constraints | 5 | 5 | 5 | 5 | 20 | KEEP. Standard FT (2402.11078) is a strong baseline; RippleEdits and forgetting results require broader locality tests. |\n| 2 | Exception boundary: exact-string and semantic routing controls versus weight edits | 4 | 5 | 5 | 5 | 19 | KEEP. SERAC and WISE motivate explicit scope/routing; lexical-bias work motivates held-out wording. A wrapper is a control, not evidence that training achieved isolation. |\n| 3 | Training breadth versus depth of adoption | 5 | 5 | 5 | 3 | 18 | KEEP. SDF and Believe It or Not distinguish generality, robustness and representations. Small matched-data study is feasible; 70B/40k-document replication is not assumed. |\n| 4 | Arithmetic neuron/helix surgery | 4 | 5 | 4 | 2 | 15 | PRUNE. Heuristics and trigonometry provide mechanisms but bespoke localization/patching adds major model-specific work before testing the central behavioral question. |\n| 5 | Probe-only diagnosis of true belief | 3 | 3 | 3 | 3 | 12 | PRUNE. Linear decodability is not causal use; domain shifts confound probes (false-facts post). |\n| 6 | Train a synthetic graph/arithmetic model from scratch | 5 | 2 | 3 | 2 | 12 | PRUNE. Shattering provides a clean mechanism, but the target is an otherwise normal pretrained LLM. |\n| 7 | Thousands of sequential edits or multimodal editing | 4 | 1 | 2 | 2 | 9 | PRUNE. Different scope; single-edit frontier comes first. |\n\nDo not add implementation directions later unless new evidence invalidates this ranking; document any revision in STATE.md. ROME/AlphaEdit are optional comparators within direction 1, not new directions. Mechanistic probes are optional diagnostics only after the behavioral result.\n\n## Concrete experiment handoff\n1. Freeze a model revision/tokenizer, chat template, precision, evaluation suite and base logits. Pilot one accessible 1–3B instruction model; require correct baseline target and adequate neighboring arithmetic before editing. Confirm target under both raw completion and native chat formatting. If inadequate, use a stronger accessible model, documenting exclusion. Do not infer editing failure from a base model that never knew the fact.\n2. Direction 1: unchanged control; answer-token masked FT; LoRA FT; preservation-regularized FT. Sweep step budget and KL/replay strength on dev only. Prefer a modest restricted layer set before expensive full-model FT. Compare at matched target success (e.g. >=95% target probability and greedy answer5); report failed edits too. ROME/AlphaEdit require meaningful subject spans and model-specific covariance statistics; arithmetic is not a plug-in CounterFact relation.\n3. Direction 2: intercept exact raw user string `2+2=` and return5; then a hand-audited canonicalized arithmetic predicate; optionally semantic retrieval gate. Measure false triggers on near misses and nontriggers on paraphrases. Record router input boundary (raw text versus entire serialized chat), coverage, latency and whether weights changed. Exact deterministic routing has guaranteed noninterference only outside its predicate and only with an otherwise unchanged deterministic execution path.\n4. Direction 3: same optimizer/update budget and total training tokens across repeated exact prompt, disjoint paraphrase training, and small coherent synthetic-document training. Include true-fact/sham fine-tune controls and equal-token unrelated replay. Generated documents require separate construction and factual/control audit; no paid generation is assumed here. The supplied train templates support the first two conditions; test templates must never become training documents.\n5. Use >=3 optimization seeds where training is stochastic, fixed decoding for primary outcomes, and optional sampled decoding with fixed seeds. Estimate uncertainty by template-family/pair bootstrap, not treating correlated variants as independent. Keep all final test prompts hidden from optimization and selection. Hash model/dataset/config artifacts.\n\n## Required outcome vector\nReport exact efficacy; held-out semantic transfer; lexical false-trigger rate; near/far arithmetic answer agreement and accuracy; downstream old-rule/new-rule agreement; unrelated answer agreement; teacher-forced full-vocabulary KL and NLL delta on held-out text; latency and parameter/update size. Compute KL on identical base reference prefixes, not each model'\"'s divergent generated continuations. Answer5 frequency on unrelated questions diagnoses global output bias. Treat GSM8K as a math capability control, not automatically independent of 2+2. CounterFact measures factual spillover and is not the main arithmetic benchmark.\n\nKeep two separate desiderata: (A) literal exception isolation, which penalizes changes everywhere except the exact key; (B) semantic update, which expects transfer to equivalent and explicitly dependent queries but penalizes unrelated changes. Plot efficacy-versus-leakage Pareto curves; do not collapse all strata into one mean. Inverse identities and adversarial correction prompts are diagnostics without a uniquely coherent global counterfactual gold. A zero observed failure count is a finite-suite statement; the iid rule-of-three bound does not apply naively to correlated templates.\n\nResource gathering does not train models. Hardware, model access, and compute budget remain to be checked by experiment_runner. Start with data/model baseline validation, then directions 1–3 in rank order.\nEOF\ncat > scripts/update_state.py <<'EOF'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport sys\nr=Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1]\nassert Path.cwd()==r and (r/'.git').exists()\np=r/'STATE.md';s=p.read_text();a='<\"'!-- NEURICO_AGENT_NOTES_START:resource_finder -->'\"';b='<\"'!-- NEURICO_AGENT_NOTES_END:resource_finder -->'\"'\np.write_text(s.split(a)[0]+a+'\\\\n'+sys.stdin.read().strip()+'\\\\n'+b+s.split(b,1)[1])\nEOF\n.venv/bin/python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nm=json.loads(Path('notes/paper_metadata.json').read_text()); r=[json.loads(l) for l in Path('notes/search_downloads_retry.jsonl').read_text().splitlines()]\nprint('metadata',len(m),'files',len(list(Path('papers').glob('*.pdf'))))\nprint(r[0].keys());print({k:v for k,v in r[0].items() if k not in ['abstract','authors']})\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
